1
00:00:00,730 --> 00:00:02,820
Here is a mammoth with a mystery

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:04,380
written into its DNA.

3
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:07,340
Two changes in a gene called FOX

4
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:10,260
Q one led scientists to predict a

5
00:00:10,260 --> 00:00:12,410
satin-like coat. They did not see

6
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:14,650
the coat, and a prediction about hair

7
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,800
is not proof that this animal lost

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:18,370
its wool or insulation.

9
00:00:18,860 --> 00:00:20,670
It lived on Wrangel Island in the

10
00:00:20,670 --> 00:00:22,780
Arctic, thousands of years ago.

11
00:00:23,190 --> 00:00:24,290
Now the strange part.

12
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:26,890
A later study of many mammoth genomes

13
00:00:27,080 --> 00:00:29,880
suggested that the isolated population had recovered

14
00:00:30,090 --> 00:00:31,480
after a severe bottleneck.

15
00:00:31,980 --> 00:00:34,380
Recovery and genetic strain could both be

16
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:37,560
true. This story comes from evidence, not

17
00:00:37,560 --> 00:00:40,080
anyone who watched the last mammoth die.

18
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,490
Bones preserve chemical signatures;

19
00:00:42,930 --> 00:00:45,810
ancient DNA preserves fragments of inheritance.

20
00:00:46,210 --> 00:00:49,430
Archaeological sites preserve traces of people, with

21
00:00:49,430 --> 00:00:52,410
gaps between them. Each witness answers a

22
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,090
different question.  So how did a population

23
00:00:55,090 --> 00:00:57,980
that survived for thousands of years disappear?

24
00:00:58,410 --> 00:00:59,780
We have four witnesses:

25
00:01:00,210 --> 00:01:03,310
a 2017 genome comparison, a 2019 study of chemistry

26
00:01:03,810 --> 00:01:08,620
and environment, the archaeological record of humans,

27
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:10,020
and a larger 2024 genome study.

28
00:01:10,380 --> 00:01:12,370
We will ask what each can actually

29
00:01:12,690 --> 00:01:16,430
tell us, what remains plausible, and what

30
00:01:16,430 --> 00:01:18,900
is missing. This is a cold case,

31
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,340
not a promise of a single convicted

32
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:21,610
killer.

33
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:28,700
Around ten thousand years ago, sea levels

34
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:31,240
rose and Arctic coastlines changed.

35
00:01:31,670 --> 00:01:34,930
A mammoth population became isolated on Wrangel

36
00:01:34,930 --> 00:01:37,250
Island, with no easy route back to

37
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:39,730
the mainland. They lived there for thousands

38
00:01:39,730 --> 00:01:42,530
of years, among the last mammoths on

39
00:01:42,530 --> 00:01:46,130
Earth.  Other populations disappeared earlier and under

40
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:49,420
different conditions. On the East European plains,

41
00:01:49,880 --> 00:01:53,020
mammoths vanished roughly fifteen thousand years ago.

42
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:56,870
On St. Paul Island in Alaska, they

43
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,390
lasted until about fifty six hundred years

44
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:02,430
ago. Isotopic changes in remains from different

45
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:05,410
regions can give researchers clues about changes

46
00:02:05,410 --> 00:02:08,020
in diet and environment, although the details

47
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:09,420
are not the same everywhere.

48
00:02:09,810 --> 00:02:12,580
Picture an island population with no easy

49
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:15,220
way to exchange animals or genes with

50
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:19,270
mainland herds. That isolation does not automatically

51
00:02:19,270 --> 00:02:21,760
doom it. The population persisted through a

52
00:02:21,760 --> 00:02:24,840
long stretch of changing Arctic history, although

53
00:02:24,840 --> 00:02:26,930
the surviving samples cannot tell us what

54
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:28,040
happened in every year.

55
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:31,200
Wrangel's sampled bones offered a different signal.

56
00:02:31,510 --> 00:02:35,500
The 2019 study found carbon and nitrogen values

57
00:02:35,500 --> 00:02:38,550
in collagen broadly stable across the sampled

58
00:02:38,550 --> 00:02:41,720
period. That is evidence about diet, not

59
00:02:41,720 --> 00:02:44,250
proof that every season was favourable or

60
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:45,720
that no animal went hungry.

61
00:02:46,220 --> 00:02:48,810
A population endured isolation for millennia.

62
00:02:49,350 --> 00:02:51,760
Its final disappearance remains unexplained.

63
00:02:52,975 --> 00:02:54,985
The first witness is DNA.

64
00:02:55,395 --> 00:02:59,895
In 2017, researchers compared two woolly mammoth genomes:

65
00:03:00,345 --> 00:03:03,155
one from mainland Siberia around forty five

66
00:03:03,155 --> 00:03:05,625
thousand years old, and one from Wrangel

67
00:03:05,625 --> 00:03:08,215
Island around forty three hundred years old.

68
00:03:08,485 --> 00:03:11,275
Two individuals are not a complete record

69
00:03:11,275 --> 00:03:14,065
of either population.  The island genome carried

70
00:03:14,065 --> 00:03:16,075
an excess of changes that could disrupt

71
00:03:16,075 --> 00:03:20,025
genes: more deletions, more deletions affecting genes,

72
00:03:20,435 --> 00:03:22,225
and more premature stop signals.

73
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:25,395
The estimated effective population size was more

74
00:03:25,395 --> 00:03:27,235
than forty three times lower than the

75
00:03:27,235 --> 00:03:30,365
mainland animal's estimate. That number is a

76
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,695
genetic estimate, not a head count.

77
00:03:32,995 --> 00:03:35,585
A premature stop can interrupt the instructions

78
00:03:35,585 --> 00:03:38,495
for a protein. Deletions can remove pieces

79
00:03:38,495 --> 00:03:40,855
of the genome. The study also reported

80
00:03:40,855 --> 00:03:43,935
losses affecting some smell-related receptors and

81
00:03:43,935 --> 00:03:47,625
urinary proteins; their biological consequences for this

82
00:03:47,625 --> 00:03:50,165
individual are not known from the sequence

83
00:03:50,165 --> 00:03:52,965
alone.  The researchers interpreted the pattern as

84
00:03:52,965 --> 00:03:54,915
consistent with genomic meltdown.

85
00:03:55,175 --> 00:03:58,165
In a small population, natural selection can

86
00:03:58,165 --> 00:04:00,815
become less effective at removing harmful variants.

87
00:04:01,235 --> 00:04:03,345
But a comparison of two genomes could

88
00:04:03,345 --> 00:04:06,075
not establish that genetic damage caused extinction.

89
00:04:06,435 --> 00:04:08,625
One detail made the stakes feel especially

90
00:04:08,625 --> 00:04:10,065
close to the animal itself.

91
00:04:11,177 --> 00:04:14,387
The Wrangel mammoth's genome contained two independent

92
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:16,677
loss of function mutations in FOX Q

93
00:04:16,677 --> 00:04:19,727
one. From comparisons with other animals, the

94
00:04:19,727 --> 00:04:21,937
authors predicted a satin-like coat.

95
00:04:22,267 --> 00:04:24,567
The coat was not directly observed.

96
00:04:24,997 --> 00:04:27,147
A predicted difference in hair structure is

97
00:04:27,147 --> 00:04:29,137
not evidence that this mammoth lost all

98
00:04:29,137 --> 00:04:32,097
its wool, lost insulation, or froze.

99
00:04:32,457 --> 00:04:34,697
That difference between a molecular trace and

100
00:04:34,697 --> 00:04:36,157
a living trait matters.

101
00:04:36,587 --> 00:04:38,657
DNA can preserve a clue when the

102
00:04:38,657 --> 00:04:40,127
animal itself is gone;

103
00:04:40,567 --> 00:04:42,367
it cannot always tell us how the

104
00:04:42,367 --> 00:04:44,857
animal looked or what happened to it.

105
00:04:45,097 --> 00:04:47,957
That distinction is easy to lose in

106
00:04:47,957 --> 00:04:51,027
a dramatic retelling. A genetic prediction can

107
00:04:51,027 --> 00:04:53,247
point toward a possible change in appearance,

108
00:04:53,637 --> 00:04:55,397
but the animal is not available for

109
00:04:55,397 --> 00:04:57,757
a coat examination, and the prediction does

110
00:04:57,757 --> 00:05:00,007
not reveal exactly how it coped with

111
00:05:00,007 --> 00:05:03,257
Arctic conditions.  The 2017 result remains a warning

112
00:05:03,257 --> 00:05:06,697
about the genetic effects of isolation.

113
00:05:07,147 --> 00:05:08,927
But it was based on one island

114
00:05:08,927 --> 00:05:11,157
genome and one mainland genome.

115
00:05:11,707 --> 00:05:14,177
Seven years later, a larger sample would

116
00:05:14,177 --> 00:05:16,397
reveal both a severe bottleneck and a

117
00:05:16,397 --> 00:05:20,207
surprising demographic recovery, without removing the possibility

118
00:05:20,207 --> 00:05:21,617
of lasting genetic strain.

119
00:05:22,748 --> 00:05:24,878
The second witness is chemistry.

120
00:05:25,288 --> 00:05:28,588
Carbon and nitrogen isotopes in collagen offer

121
00:05:28,588 --> 00:05:32,018
clues about diet; sulfur and strontium can

122
00:05:32,018 --> 00:05:34,528
also provide evidence about water and local

123
00:05:34,528 --> 00:05:39,358
geology. In 2019 researchers compared remains from Wrangel

124
00:05:39,548 --> 00:05:40,928
and other northern regions.

125
00:05:41,308 --> 00:05:43,998
The Wrangel carbon and nitrogen values remained

126
00:05:43,998 --> 00:05:46,338
broadly stable in the samples they studied.

127
00:05:46,798 --> 00:05:49,658
That weighs against some gradual dietary changes.

128
00:05:50,188 --> 00:05:51,618
It does not prove the herd was

129
00:05:51,618 --> 00:05:54,058
healthy or that the island always offered

130
00:05:54,058 --> 00:05:57,138
easy conditions.  Isotope measurements are records of

131
00:05:57,138 --> 00:05:59,898
particular animals and tissues, not a weather

132
00:05:59,898 --> 00:06:01,688
station reporting every storm.

133
00:06:01,868 --> 00:06:05,158
A stable sampled dietary signal can coexist

134
00:06:05,158 --> 00:06:07,788
with seasonal stress, and a rapid event

135
00:06:07,788 --> 00:06:09,598
could escape a record that is sparse

136
00:06:09,598 --> 00:06:12,828
in time.  Sulfur and strontium patterns were

137
00:06:12,828 --> 00:06:16,118
interpreted as consistent with increased bedrock weathering

138
00:06:16,118 --> 00:06:17,978
toward the end of the island population's

139
00:06:17,978 --> 00:06:20,528
history. The authors suggested this might have

140
00:06:20,528 --> 00:06:22,398
affected drinking water quality.

141
00:06:22,768 --> 00:06:25,018
It is an inference, not a directly

142
00:06:25,018 --> 00:06:26,778
tested glass of ancient water.

143
00:06:27,128 --> 00:06:29,028
They also proposed a rapid threat:

144
00:06:29,468 --> 00:06:32,108
rain falling on snow and freezing, making

145
00:06:32,108 --> 00:06:33,198
plants hard to reach.

146
00:06:33,608 --> 00:06:35,508
An icing event could harm a small

147
00:06:35,508 --> 00:06:37,668
herd quickly. But no one observed a

148
00:06:37,668 --> 00:06:41,438
fatal Wrangel winter. Genetic strain, water and

149
00:06:41,438 --> 00:06:43,468
weather may have interacted;

150
00:06:43,918 --> 00:06:46,738
that scenario remains a hypothesis, not a

151
00:06:46,738 --> 00:06:47,618
solved case.

152
00:06:48,754 --> 00:06:50,424
The third witness is us.

153
00:06:50,894 --> 00:06:53,334
People hunted mammoths elsewhere, so it is

154
00:06:53,334 --> 00:06:55,944
reasonable to ask whether humans reached Wrangel

155
00:06:56,214 --> 00:06:57,794
while the animals were still alive.

156
00:06:58,354 --> 00:07:00,964
The earliest known archaeological evidence of people

157
00:07:00,964 --> 00:07:02,974
on the island dates to around thirty

158
00:07:02,974 --> 00:07:06,304
six hundred calibrated years before present, several

159
00:07:06,304 --> 00:07:09,164
centuries after the youngest known mammoth remains.

160
00:07:09,564 --> 00:07:11,904
The earliest evidence is not necessarily the

161
00:07:11,904 --> 00:07:16,354
first arrival. The 2019 researchers cautioned that a

162
00:07:16,354 --> 00:07:19,204
human contribution could not be ruled out,

163
00:07:19,574 --> 00:07:22,424
because direct evidence of an encounter would

164
00:07:22,424 --> 00:07:23,734
be difficult to find.

165
00:07:24,204 --> 00:07:27,494
Elsewhere, archaeological finds show that humans and

166
00:07:27,494 --> 00:07:30,854
mammoths interacted, but evidence from another place

167
00:07:31,124 --> 00:07:33,914
cannot automatically be transferred to this island.

168
00:07:34,164 --> 00:07:35,594
Here we have to keep the known

169
00:07:35,594 --> 00:07:38,154
dates separate from possibilities that the record

170
00:07:38,154 --> 00:07:41,084
has not resolved.  So human involvement is

171
00:07:41,084 --> 00:07:43,354
possible, but the known dates do not

172
00:07:43,354 --> 00:07:45,794
establish it. The apparent gap is not

173
00:07:45,794 --> 00:07:48,054
proof of innocence, and it is not

174
00:07:48,054 --> 00:07:49,364
proof of hunting either.

175
00:07:49,844 --> 00:07:51,974
Three witnesses have offered clues;

176
00:07:52,404 --> 00:07:54,424
none has shown the final moment.

177
00:07:55,374 --> 00:07:59,604
In 2024, researchers published a wider genetic record:

178
00:08:00,024 --> 00:08:04,204
twenty one Siberian woolly mammoth genomes, including

179
00:08:04,204 --> 00:08:07,974
seven mainland animals from before isolation and

180
00:08:07,974 --> 00:08:10,134
fourteen from the Wrangel population.

181
00:08:10,564 --> 00:08:12,364
The bottleneck was severe.

182
00:08:12,794 --> 00:08:17,034
Heterozygosity, one measure of genetic diversity, fell

183
00:08:17,034 --> 00:08:20,124
by more than forty percent after isolation.

184
00:08:20,504 --> 00:08:23,864
Long runs of homozygosity, stretches with matching

185
00:08:23,864 --> 00:08:27,204
inherited copies, rose from roughly ten percent

186
00:08:27,474 --> 00:08:29,094
to about forty two percent of the

187
00:08:29,094 --> 00:08:33,044
genome. Diversity in the major histocompatibility complex,

188
00:08:33,484 --> 00:08:35,244
a group of genes involved in immune

189
00:08:35,244 --> 00:08:38,924
responses, declined more than genome-wide diversity.

190
00:08:39,394 --> 00:08:42,454
Homozygosity and genetic diversity measure related but

191
00:08:42,454 --> 00:08:44,144
different aspects of the genome.

192
00:08:44,664 --> 00:08:46,374
Neither is a direct count of sick

193
00:08:46,374 --> 00:08:49,234
animals. The loss of immune-gene diversity

194
00:08:49,234 --> 00:08:51,874
is a genetic finding, not a diagnosis

195
00:08:51,874 --> 00:08:53,174
of a specific infection.

196
00:08:53,524 --> 00:08:54,644
Then came the surprise.

197
00:08:55,054 --> 00:08:58,364
Modelling suggested that effective population size fell

198
00:08:58,364 --> 00:09:00,194
to eight or fewer at the bottleneck,

199
00:09:00,664 --> 00:09:02,924
then recovered to around two hundred to

200
00:09:02,924 --> 00:09:05,794
three hundred within roughly twenty generations.

201
00:09:06,264 --> 00:09:09,294
Remember: effective size is a genetic model

202
00:09:09,294 --> 00:09:12,264
parameter, not a census of eight mammoths

203
00:09:12,264 --> 00:09:15,444
becoming three hundred. Across the years represented

204
00:09:15,444 --> 00:09:18,744
by the samples, the population appeared comparatively

205
00:09:18,744 --> 00:09:21,804
stable. Recovery did not mean its genetic

206
00:09:21,804 --> 00:09:23,234
risks disappeared.

207
00:09:24,371 --> 00:09:27,131
Diversity and homozygosity are not the only

208
00:09:27,131 --> 00:09:28,821
things researchers can compare.

209
00:09:29,221 --> 00:09:31,801
They can also examine variants predicted to

210
00:09:31,801 --> 00:09:34,511
be harmful. But ancient genomes differ in

211
00:09:34,511 --> 00:09:36,371
how much of their sequence is readable,

212
00:09:36,651 --> 00:09:38,821
so coverage and data quality matter.

213
00:09:39,231 --> 00:09:41,631
Imagine looking into the same room through

214
00:09:41,631 --> 00:09:43,491
windows of different sizes.

215
00:09:43,941 --> 00:09:46,461
The larger window might reveal more damage

216
00:09:46,461 --> 00:09:48,121
even if neither room is worse.

217
00:09:48,661 --> 00:09:51,061
The comparison matters because a shallow sequence

218
00:09:51,381 --> 00:09:53,521
may miss changes that are visible in

219
00:09:53,521 --> 00:09:54,751
a better-covered genome.

220
00:09:55,111 --> 00:09:57,791
Without testing that bias, a difference in

221
00:09:57,791 --> 00:09:59,551
the data could be mistaken for a

222
00:09:59,551 --> 00:10:01,081
difference between the animals.

223
00:10:01,621 --> 00:10:04,031
After accounting for these differences, the 2024 team

224
00:10:04,031 --> 00:10:05,921
found two opposing trends.

225
00:10:06,501 --> 00:10:10,151
Variants predicted to have the highest impact

226
00:10:10,381 --> 00:10:13,611
became less common, consistent with natural selection

227
00:10:13,611 --> 00:10:14,561
purging some of them.

228
00:10:15,051 --> 00:10:19,021
Moderately harmful variants accumulated, consistent with genetic

229
00:10:19,021 --> 00:10:22,841
drift outpacing selection. These are predicted effects,

230
00:10:23,301 --> 00:10:25,901
not medical examinations of individual mammoths.

231
00:10:26,251 --> 00:10:28,361
The sampled genomes did not show a

232
00:10:28,361 --> 00:10:31,651
clear accelerating genomic collapse across their history.

233
00:10:32,141 --> 00:10:34,921
Yet the youngest genome predates the final

234
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:38,701
generations. A population can recover demographically and

235
00:10:38,701 --> 00:10:40,771
still carry lasting genetic strain.

236
00:10:41,221 --> 00:10:44,251
Neither finding, by itself, tells us what

237
00:10:44,251 --> 00:10:45,441
caused the extinction.

238
00:10:46,471 --> 00:10:48,181
Here is the missing witness.

239
00:10:48,741 --> 00:10:51,711
The 2024 genome dataset contains no samples from

240
00:10:51,711 --> 00:10:56,471
the final ten generations, roughly three centuries.

241
00:10:56,831 --> 00:10:59,581
Its youngest genome dates to around forty

242
00:10:59,581 --> 00:11:02,681
three hundred calibrated years before present.

243
00:11:03,091 --> 00:11:05,281
That gap is in the genomic dataset;

244
00:11:05,711 --> 00:11:07,271
it does not mean there is no

245
00:11:07,271 --> 00:11:08,861
other evidence from that period.

246
00:11:09,251 --> 00:11:10,981
We can study much of the setup,

247
00:11:11,401 --> 00:11:12,821
but we do not have a complete

248
00:11:12,821 --> 00:11:14,451
genetic record of the ending.

249
00:11:14,851 --> 00:11:17,101
And a newer lead shows why careful

250
00:11:17,101 --> 00:11:21,881
questions matter. In 2025, researchers studied host-associated

251
00:11:21,881 --> 00:11:25,261
microbial DNA across hundreds of mammoth specimens,

252
00:11:25,511 --> 00:11:28,161
including material far older than the Wrangel

253
00:11:28,161 --> 00:11:31,261
population. Some bacteria were related to known

254
00:11:31,261 --> 00:11:34,671
pathogens.  The study included hundreds of specimens,

255
00:11:35,091 --> 00:11:37,391
not just the last island herd, and

256
00:11:37,391 --> 00:11:39,661
reached far back into mammoth history.

257
00:11:40,071 --> 00:11:42,561
Its microbial traces are another line of

258
00:11:42,561 --> 00:11:45,621
investigation, not a direct witness to the

259
00:11:45,621 --> 00:11:46,911
last day on Wrangel.

260
00:11:47,381 --> 00:11:49,471
Related does not mean responsible.

261
00:11:49,771 --> 00:11:51,691
The study did not identify a disease

262
00:11:51,691 --> 00:11:53,891
that killed the Wrangel mammoths, and the

263
00:11:53,891 --> 00:11:56,411
health effects of the ancient microbes remain

264
00:11:56,411 --> 00:12:01,341
uncertain. Authentication, contamination control and comparisons with

265
00:12:01,341 --> 00:12:03,831
living relatives can open new questions.

266
00:12:04,291 --> 00:12:06,291
The diagnosis is still missing.

267
00:12:07,307 --> 00:12:08,187
What can we say?

268
00:12:08,767 --> 00:12:11,497
Supported: the island population went through a

269
00:12:11,497 --> 00:12:14,837
severe bottleneck, followed by a modelled recovery.

270
00:12:15,107 --> 00:12:18,827
The sampled genomes show reduced diversity, increased

271
00:12:18,827 --> 00:12:21,657
homozygosity and persistent genetic strain.

272
00:12:22,077 --> 00:12:25,967
Important dietary isotope indicators were broadly stable

273
00:12:25,967 --> 00:12:28,797
in the samples; other signals were interpreted

274
00:12:28,797 --> 00:12:31,157
as possible changes in water quality.

275
00:12:31,607 --> 00:12:34,947
Plausible, not proven: a severe icing event,

276
00:12:35,297 --> 00:12:39,247
disease, poorer water, human involvement, or a

277
00:12:39,247 --> 00:12:42,107
combination of pressures affecting a small isolated

278
00:12:42,107 --> 00:12:46,117
population. Not established: a single named killer,

279
00:12:46,567 --> 00:12:49,397
a directly recorded fatal winter, or a

280
00:12:49,397 --> 00:12:51,547
genetic test proving the cause.

281
00:12:51,947 --> 00:12:54,367
The final generations are absent from the

282
00:12:54,367 --> 00:12:57,817
larger genome dataset.  A possibility is not

283
00:12:57,817 --> 00:13:00,137
a verdict. The evidence does not show

284
00:13:00,337 --> 00:13:02,447
the herd was always healthy, that people

285
00:13:02,447 --> 00:13:04,867
definitely arrived too late to matter, or

286
00:13:04,867 --> 00:13:06,857
that the final decline followed one known

287
00:13:06,857 --> 00:13:09,227
pattern.  These categories do not have to

288
00:13:09,227 --> 00:13:12,217
compete. Vulnerability and a sudden event could

289
00:13:12,217 --> 00:13:14,897
work together, but that is a hypothesis,

290
00:13:15,217 --> 00:13:17,297
not the answer to this particular case.

291
00:13:17,727 --> 00:13:20,997
The 2017 genome, the isotope record, archaeology and

292
00:13:22,037 --> 00:13:24,577
the 2024 genomes each provide different pieces.

293
00:13:24,577 --> 00:13:26,977
A dramatic clue is not the same

294
00:13:26,977 --> 00:13:28,857
thing as a cause.

295
00:13:28,857 --> 00:13:32,997
For small, isolated populations alive today, recovery

296
00:13:32,997 --> 00:13:37,247
can be encouraging without erasing every genetic

297
00:13:37,247 --> 00:13:40,277
risk. For the Wrangel mammoths, we know

298
00:13:40,277 --> 00:13:42,167
more about the long journey than the

299
00:13:42,167 --> 00:13:44,657
final moment. The last question is not

300
00:13:44,657 --> 00:13:45,867
just what killed them.

301
00:13:46,367 --> 00:13:48,047
It is what evidence we are still

302
00:13:48,047 --> 00:13:48,547
missing.
